  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=4+9\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=4,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=9,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{\u2015}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+{9}^{2} \\\\  & =16+81 \\\\  & =97.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=4+9\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{\u2015}{z}=4-9\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{\u2015}{z} & =(4+9\\text{i})(4-9\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+(4)(-9)\\text{i}+(4)(9)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(9)^{2} \\\\  & =16-36\\text{i}+36\\text{i}-(-1)(81) \\\\  & =16+81 \\\\  & =97.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
